ains only required items in original order.\n    one_minimal = True\n    return {\"attempts\": attempts, \"one_minimal\": one_minimal, \"reduced_items\": reduced}\n\nBut note: \"reduced_items\" should be an array of strings in some order. The order should presumably be the original order of items with non-required removed. The above preserves order.\n\nNow, is there any case where an item in required might appear multiple times? Not in fixtures. But if duplicates exist, the required set would treat them as unique. The algorithm should handle that: if required items have duplicates, we need to keep all occurrences? The fixture doesn't show duplicates. For safety, we could keep the required items as they appear in items (maybe multiple copies). The problem statement doesn't specify. But using a set loses duplicates. However, the required_failure_items list might have duplicates? Unlikely. The name suggests a set. I'll assume no duplicates.\n\nAlso, what about items that are not in items list? Assume valid.\n\nThus the implementation is straightforward.\n\nBut we must ensure that the output matches the schema exactly: \"attempts\" integer, \"one_minimal\" boolean, \"reduced_items\" array of strings.\n\nOne more consideration: The case argument might have other fields? The spec says use only fields present in payload object. So we use items and required_failure_items. The _case_nonce is also present but we ignore.\n\nNow, we need to output a single JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a JSON string. The source code must define solve(case). No external imports, only standard library.\n\nLet's write the code. We'll include proper escaping for JSON.\n\nFinal answer:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    items = case['items']\\n    required = set(case['required_failure_items'])\\n    attempts = 0\\n    reduced = []\\n    for item in items:\\n        if item in required:\\n            reduced.append(item)\\n        else:\\n      